 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the town law, in relation to authorizing the town board of the town of Rockland, in the county of Sullivan, to diminish the area of the Roscoe sewer district",
    "basePrintNo": "S6677",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6677-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §202-c, Town L",
    "lawSection": "Town Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6677              REVISED 04/27/10\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the town law, in relation to authorizing the town\nboard of the town of Rockland, in the county of Sullivan, to diminish\nthe area of the Roscoe sewer district\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nTo allow the Town of Rockland to diminish the Roscoe Sewer District by\n1/2 acre.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nThe bill authorizes the Town of Rockland to diminish the Roscoe Sewer\nDistrict to the extent of the metes and bounds description provided\nfor in the legislation. The total area the district would be\ndiminished would be 1/2 acre.\n\n EFFECTS OF PRESENT LAW WHICH THIS BILL WOULD ALTER :\nSection 202-c (1-a) of the Town Law (New York State Law) allows the\nabolition of unimproved sewer districts but does not allow the\ndiminishment of existing districts, whether improved or not.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nThe sewer district was erroneously formed with the 1/2 acre parcel in\nit. The parcel in question is separated from the rest of the district\nby a creek. In order to service that property, in addition to crossing\nunder the stream, pumping would be required in order to create\nadequate flow. The Town of Rockland advises such a plan is not\nfeasible and that it was error to include the property in the District\nin the first place.  The Town of Rockland has requested this\nlegislation. The legislation is requested so that the property which\nis not being benefited by the sewer district can be taken out of it,\nthereby allowing the Town to stop taxing it.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nNew Bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n None.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
